{
 "thread": "kkCRuJGYsVc",
 "subject": "Robustness of spatially explicit JSSA models",
 "messages_listed": 4,
 "last_activity": "2024-11-06T20:02:11Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "B0ThS7nABQAJ",
   "from": "jcl...@utk.edu",
   "author": "5a91d3696b12d600",
   "date": "2024-11-05T14:32:21Z",
   "subject": "Robustness of spatially explicit JSSA models",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "I am using an open secr model (PLBsecrl) to estimate phi and lambda across a number of years.  I also have estimates of abundance using a closed secr model, from which I can estimate lambda by hand.  I want to say that the PLBsecrl model is more robust to assumption violations (e.g., non-homogenous capture probabilities) than the closed secr model for estimating lambda, because N (which is greatly affected by such assumption violations) does not enter into the equation in the latter.  The Efford and Schofield (2020) paper seems to say this but I just wanted to make sure I am reading that correctly.  Thanks for any feedback!<div>Joe</div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "RpJjvfVzBQAJ",
   "from": "Murray Efford",
   "author": "118cea91a1619106",
   "date": "2024-11-05T21:16:43Z",
   "subject": "Re: Robustness of spatially explicit JSSA models",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div>Hi Joe</div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;m not sure this is right. I can&#39;t put my finger on where the 2020 paper that might support what you suggest. <br></div><div><br></div><div>Closed-population multi-session analyses can get quite sophisticated - have you seen <a href=\"https://www.otago.ac.nz/density/pdfs/secr-trend.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.otago.ac.nz/density/pdfs/secr-trend.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792486275419000&amp;usg=AOvVaw26MMe_HQ2Y1vJmi6dIqoOo\">https://www.otago.ac.nz/density/pdfs/secr-trend.pdf</a>?</div><div><br></div><div>A relevant non-spatial paper is Hines &amp; Nichols (2002) &#39;Investigations of potential bias in the estimation of lambda using Pradel&#39;s (1996) model for capture-recapture<br>data&#39; Journal of Applied Statistics, 29:1-4, 573-587. That makes the point that for non-spatial CR, N-hat and therefore lambda-hat is sensitive to extent of sampling - this is one benefit of using SECR, whether open- or closed-.<br></div><div><br></div><div>Murray<br></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"auto\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Wednesday, November 6, 2024 at 3:32:21 AM UTC+13 <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">jcl...@utk.edu</a> wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">I am using an open secr model (PLBsecrl) to estimate phi and lambda across a number of years.  I also have estimates of abundance using a closed secr model, from which I can estimate lambda by hand.  I want to say that the PLBsecrl model is more robust to assumption violations (e.g., non-homogenous capture probabilities) than the closed secr model for estimating lambda, because N (which is greatly affected by such assumption violations) does not enter into the equation in the latter.  The Efford and Schofield (2020) paper seems to say this but I just wanted to make sure I am reading that correctly.  Thanks for any feedback!<div>Joe</div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
