confidence interval coverage (Table </span><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;margin:0px;padding:0px;border:0px;font-size:15px;vertical-align:baseline;color:rgb(42,42,42);font-variant-numeric:inherit;font-variant-east-asian:inherit;font-variant-alternates:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;line-height:inherit;font-size-adjust:inherit;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit\"></span><a style=\"box-sizing:border-box;margin:0px;padding:0px;border:0px;font-variant-numeric:inherit;font-variant-east-asian:inherit;font-variant-alternates:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:15px;line-height:inherit;font-size-adjust:inherit;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;vertical-align:baseline;color:rgb(0,111,183);word-break:break-word\" rel=\"nofollow\">4</a><span style=\"color:rgb(42,42,42);font-size:15px\">).&quot;  The table then shows some of the biases of other models (including spatially explicit closed).  However, I think I see now that you were referring to the reduction of capture heterogeneity based on the spatial location of the activity centers would produce a more robust estimate than the non-spatially explicit estimate.  </span></font><div><font face=\"Arial\"><font color=\"#2a2a2a\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">I had not seen the note on estimating lambda directly in closed secr models.  That is exactly what I need!  Thank you.</span></font></font></div><div><font face=\"Arial\"><font color=\"#2a2a2a\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Joe<br></span></font></font><br></div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"auto\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Tuesday, November 5, 2024 at 4:16:43 PM UTC-5 <a rel=\"nofollow\">murray...@gmail.com</a> wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div>Hi Joe</div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;m not sure this is right. I can&#39;t put my finger on where the 2020 paper that might support what you suggest. <br></div><div><br></div><div>Closed-population multi-session analyses can get quite sophisticated - have you seen <a href=\"https://www.otago.ac.nz/density/pdfs/secr-trend.pdf\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.otago.ac.nz/density/pdfs/secr-trend.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792486275425000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0DSPkA_cFmNbwpFFEQEDvv\">https://www.otago.ac.nz/density/pdfs/secr-trend.pdf</a>?</div><div><br></div><div>A relevant non-spatial paper is Hines &amp; Nichols (2002) &#39;Investigations of potential bias in the estimation of lambda using Pradel&#39;s (1996) model for capture-recapture<br>data&#39; Journal of Applied Statistics, 29:1-4, 573-587. That makes the point that for non-spatial CR, N-hat and therefore lambda-hat is sensitive to extent of sampling - this is one benefit of using SECR, whether open- or closed-.<br></div><div><br></div><div>Murray<br></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"auto\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Wednesday, November 6, 2024 at 3:32:21 AM UTC+13 <a rel=\"nofollow\">jcl...@utk.edu</a> wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">I am using an open secr model (PLBsecrl) to estimate phi and lambda across a number of years.  I also have estimates of abundance using a closed secr model, from which I can estimate lambda by hand.  I want to say that the PLBsecrl model is more robust to assumption violations (e.g., non-homogenous capture probabilities) than the closed secr model for estimating lambda, because N (which is greatly affected by such assumption violations) does not enter into the equation in the latter.  The Efford and Schofield (2020) paper seems to say this but I just wanted to make sure I am reading that correctly.  Thanks for any feedback!<div>Joe</div></blockquote></div></blockquote></div></blockquote></div>"
